The next formula is presented without an assumed named law (1131). [let series]
\begin{equation}q_{1331}\end{equation}

The equation $i=V/R$ is a resistor current law, not a capacitor derivative law.

The next formula is presented without an assumed named law (1139). [define as]
\begin{equation}q_{1339}\end{equation}

Current is declared leaving the positive terminal, yet the document asserts $i=C\,dv/dt$.

The next formula is presented without an assumed named law (1147). [let series]
\begin{equation}q_{1347}\end{equation}

The candidate \[i=C\int_0^t v(\tau)\,d\tau\] integrates voltage instead of differentiating it.
